%T A401980 1068,1069,1883,1884,3313,3314,5815,5816,10169,10170,17686,17687,
%U A401980 30539,30540,52290,52291,88731,88732,149228,149229,248868,248869,411883,411884
%N A401980 Number of tilings of a strip of length n using squares (length 1) and dominoes (length 2) such that each domino has a prime number of squares preceding it.
%F A401980 a(n) = 1 + Sum_{d=1..floor(n/2), pi(n-2*d)!=0} binomial(d+pi(n-2*d)-1, d), where pi(m) counts primes <= m.
%F A401980 G.f.: A(x) = Sum_{s>=0} x^s/(1-x^2)^pi(s), where pi(s) is the prime-counting function.
%e A401980 For n = 6, we can tile using 0, 1, or 2 dominoes. 0 dominoes: 1 tiling (all squares). 1 domino: 2 valid starting positions (2 squares or 3 squares in front). 2 dominoes: 1 tiling (SSDD). In total, there are 4 possible tilings.
